      "key": "ux_method",
      "label": "UX Method"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The Elm Architecture process model consists of runtime-held typed State; a typed Msg algebra naming every user or system action; pure Update : (Msg, State) → (State, Cmd); pure View : State → ViewTree with identical output for identical state; and typed Cmd/Sub descriptions of scheduled effects and external subscriptions interpreted at the runtime's effect membrane. The runtime owns the render, message, update and command-interpretation loop; Update describes effects without performing them.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 157,
          "quote": "- **State** — typed state shape held by the runtime, not by the view\n- **Msg** — typed message algebra naming every action a user or system can emit\n- **Update** — pure reducer of signature `(Msg, State) → (State, Cmd)`; describes side effects as `Cmd` values, does not perform them\n- **View** — pure function of signature `State → ViewTree`; given the same state, produces the same output\n- **Cmd / Sub** — typed descriptions of side effects to schedule and external sources to subscribe to; interpreted by the runtime at the effect membrane\n\nThe runtime owns the loop:\n\n`current State → render View → user emits Msg → Update produces new State and Cmds → runtime interprets Cmds → repeat`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/UX_METHOD.md#UX%20Method%2F4.%20Process%20Model%20%E2%80%94%20Elm%20Architecture",
          "start_line": 149
        }
      ],
      "key": "elm_architecture",
      "label": "Elm Architecture"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "World Model Method is the named constitutional method surface for world-model work. Its introductory marker alone supplies no Product adoption or operation grant.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 3,
          "quote": "# World Model Method\n\n<!-- STDO3_PROFILE_BINDING_START -->",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method",
          "start_line": 1
        }
      ],
      "key": "world_model_method",
      "label": "World Model Method"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "World-model work builds inspectable, versioned, machine-reasonable domain truth as a governed semantic layer over authoritative source systems. These systems enact bounded domain function through code, events, records, APIs, schedules, configurations, documents and downstream obligations. Domains are bounded by function and authority; records require function, provenance and treatment context; mappings are semantic treatments; catalogs and schemas explain deeper semantics. The model traces authoritative surfaces, records accepted claims in ledgers and publishes bounded cuts that support probabilistic reasoning.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 74,
          "quote": "World-model work builds a governed semantic layer over authoritative source\nsystems.\n\nThose systems enact bounded domain function through code paths, events,\nrecords, APIs, schedules, configurations, documents, and downstream\nobligations.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FPosition",
          "start_line": 69
        }
      ],
      "key": "world_model",
      "label": "World model"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Source Project is the SPEC-owned concept urn:stdo:concept:recursive-product-taxonomy:source-project: a mutable workspace building the next release cut. It remains distinct from a released Product, stamped Install and published Artifact; PRODUCT.md is its product-definition surface.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 154,
          "quote": "- `Source Project` retains the mutable-workspace-building-the-next-release-cut\n  meaning;\n- `Artifact` retains the published-output meaning; and\n- `PRODUCT.md` remains the product-definition surface of a mutable Source\n  Project, not the released Product artifact itself.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FMethod%20Role",
          "start_line": 150
        }
      ],
      "key": "source_project",
      "label": "Source Project"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The world-model method's two constitutional intents are building published domain artifacts and composing them into higher-order world models. Query, traversal and serving are important delivery concerns, potentially realized later through dedicated planes, but do not define that constitutional identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 205,
          "quote": "World-model work has two constitutional intents:\n\n1. build published domain artifacts\n2. compose those artifacts into higher-order world models\n\nQuery, traversal, and serving over those artifacts are important delivery\nconcerns, but they do not define the constitutional identity of the method.\n\nThose concerns may later be realized through a dedicated query or serving plane\nwithout changing the two core intents above.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FConstitutional%20Intents",
          "start_line": 196
        }
      ],
      "key": "world_model_intents",
      "label": "World-model constitutional intents"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A Markov object is a substrate-neutral, stable self-bounding pattern in a constraint structure whose internal state can be reasoned about through an effective blanket. The source proposes, as a working claim rather than a demonstrated universal result, that this topology propagates across biological systems, natural-language traces, learned representations and engineered institutional systems, permitting explicit, traceable and publishable recovery of expert-recognized objects.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 299,
          "quote": "A Markov object is a stable self-bounding pattern in a constraint\nstructure whose internal state can be reasoned about through its\neffective blanket. The term is substrate-neutral. The same topology is\nexpected wherever a constraint structure admits stable configurations:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FTheoretical%20Underpinnings%20Of%20The%20Markov%20Object",
          "start_line": 296
        }
      ],
      "key": "markov_object_construct",
      "label": "Markov-object construct"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["The geometric reading concerns identity in a representation space."],
      "definition": "An effective blanket is read as a low-rank projection along which identity survives treatment, rather than a crisp partition of attribute columns. Attribute bases distribute identity over partially aligned atoms; overwriting apparently external attributes can transfer partial identity. A published geometric overlay combines an identity direction with its distributed evidence. Reported direction-level behavioral evidence is candidate evidence; the formal conditional-independence predicate was tested and unmet.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 342,
          "quote": "- The effective blanket is a low-rank projection in the representation\n  space along which object identity is preserved under treatment.\n- The attribute basis is evidence for that projection. The projection\n  is not a column-membership predicate.\n- A published Markov-object overlay is an identity direction together\n  with the distributed attribute evidence supporting it.\n\nThis reading is consistent with candidate evidence from a learned\nrepresentation system. The evidence is direction-level and\nbehavioural. The formal conditional-independence condition that would\npromote the Markov-object construct from candidate to established has\nbeen tested and did not meet its predicate. The construct remains\ncandidate-class. See *Epistemic Status Of The Construct*.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FTheoretical%20Underpinnings%20Of%20The%20Markov%20Object%2FWhat%20An%20Effective%20Blanket%20Actually%20Is",
          "start_line": 330
        }
      ],
      "key": "effective_blanket",
      "label": "Effective blanket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A domain is a coherent functional surface defined by its inputs, changes, ownership, guarantees, outputs and what adjacent domains can rely on.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 512,
          "quote": "A domain is defined by coherent function:\n\n- what enters\n- what changes\n- what is owned\n- what is guaranteed\n- what leaves\n- what adjacent domains can rely on",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FCore%20Claims%2FDomains%20Are%20Functional%20Surfaces",
          "start_line": 505
