    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The Markov Object unit is a stable self-bounding world-model object understood through its effective blanket. Its published form is described as an immutable ledger-projected identity direction with distributed evidence, not mutable record or column enumeration. This is a theoretical account of recovered, already institutionally committed entities, not invention by the builder. A recovered entity publishes as an Entity Cut; geometric representation may additionally support an overlay.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 702,
          "quote": "A recovered entity is published as an **Entity Cut**. Where the builder\nadditionally operates over a representation space in which identity is a\ngeometric direction, the entity cut may carry a Markov-object overlay.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FMethod%20Units%2FMarkov%20Object",
          "start_line": 700
        }
      ],
      "key": "markov_object_unit",
      "label": "Markov Object unit"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Entity Cut is the primary published semantic unit: an identity-bearing, bounded, evidence-backed ledger projection of a recovered entity. It is lawful without an identity direction and publishes identity, boundary, authority, state, evidence, treatment, covariance and ambiguity, each traced to ledger entries and assurance. Initial loose candidates converge into entity cuts or remain explicitly non-object artifacts such as treatment surfaces, covariance edges or temporal references.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
